  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1201 AM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nToday will be last day for a while that will not see any type of\nweather other than \"chamber of commerce\" weather. Changes start\non Friday, although it mainly will be stronger winds. Warm\ntemperatures and dry conditions will remain in place one more\nday, and in fact tomorrow looks to be a bit warmer in some\nspots. Gusts tomorrow will reach 25-35 mph over much of central\nand eastern Idaho, with some gusts in the central mountains\ntopping out around 40 mph. At the moment, that wouldn't be\npushing any wind-related headlines outside of fire weather. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms begin to increase Saturday and will\ncontinue into early next week. All versions of the GFS and ECMWF\nare showing split flow across the West through early next\nweek. The main low drops across and offshore of California\nbefore swinging east. The signal is still there that ahead of\nthat, remnants of Rachel are pulled north and impact portions of\ncentral and eastern Idaho. The latest forecast has 1-2\" of\nprecipitation across the South Hills/Albion Mountains,\nsoutheast and eastern highlands. Higher ridgelines east of I-15\nare pushing 2.25-2.5\". There is some spillover into the Snake \nPlain of 1.0-1.5\" from Pocatello up through Rexburg. Amounts \nover the eastern and southeast highlands are certainly pushing \nthe highest end of forecast amounts in our Blend of Models but \ncertainly possible given the pattern and tropical moisture. \nAmounts taper off across the central mountains, where some of \nthe \"drier\" valley spots are in the 0.10-0.30\" range. There is \nstill some uncertainty on how far west the moisture Rachel \nactually reaches, so we may see some wobbling back and forth \nuntil the weekend on where higher amounts may fall. It will be \ncold enough for accumulating snow above 7000ft across the \ncentral mountains and along the Divide. Amounts are fairly \nlight, but could amount to several inches for the highest peaks \nin the central mountains and over toward Island Park. With \ncolder temperatures Sunday and Monday, we could see snowflakes \nfalling much lower than that...BUT may not stick. We will likely\nend up with more widespread frost/freeze issues on mornings \nwhere precipitation is not expected or is minimal. For the \nmiddle of next week, another low tries to drop into the western \nstates. This would keep cooler air in place and keep the \npotential for precipitation going.",
